{"title":"VITEEE","category":"Engineering","bank":"viteee","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Kinetic Theory of Gases","count":7,"questions":[{"id":117569,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Kinetic Theory of Gases","exam":"Engineering","examName":"VITEEE","q":"If $\\bar{v}, v_{\\mathrm{rms}}$ and $v_{p}$ represent the mean speed, root mean square and most probable speed of the molecules in an ideal monoatomic gas at temperature $T$ and if $m$ is mass of the molecule, then","options":["$\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{p}} < \\mathrm{v} < \\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{rms}}$","$no molecule can have a speed greater than \\sqrt{2} v_{r m s}$","no molecule can have a speed less than $v_{p} / \\sqrt{2}$","None of the above"],"answer":0,"solution":"Mean speed, $\\overline{\\mathrm{v}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{8 \\mathrm{kT}}{\\pi \\mathrm{m}}}=0.92 \\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\mathrm{rms}$ speed, $\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{3 \\mathrm{kT}}{\\mathrm{m}}}$\nMost probable speed $v_{p}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\mathrm{kT}}{\\mathrm{m}}}=0.816 \\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{rms}}$\ni.e., $\\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{p}} < \\overline{\\mathrm{v}} < \\mathrm{v}_{\\mathrm{rms}}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"VITEEE 2013","_marksId":"64e45900ec226f2f4360a7f9"},{"id":117570,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Kinetic Theory of Gases","exam":"Engineering","examName":"VITEEE","q":"$50 \\Omega$ and $100 \\Omega$ resistors are connected in series. This connection is connected with a battery of $2.4 \\mathrm{ V}$. When a voltmeter of $100 \\Omega$ resistance is connected across $100 \\Omega$ resistor, then the reading of the voltmeter will be","options":["$1.6 \\mathrm{ V}$","$1.0 \\mathrm{ V}$","$1.2 \\mathrm{ V}$","$2.0 \\mathrm{ V}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Equivalent resistance of the circuit R_{e q}=100 \\Omega Current through the circuit, $i=\\frac{V}{R}=\\frac{2.4}{100} \\mathrm{ A}$\nPotential difference across combination of voltmeter and $100 \\Omega$ resistance =$\\frac{2.4}{100} \\times50=1.2 \\mathrm{ V}$ Since the voltmeter and $100 \\Omega$ resistance are in parallel, the voltmeter reads the same value i.e., $1.2 \\mathrm{ V}$.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"VITEEE 2010","_marksId":"64e458ddec226f2f4360a69c"},{"id":117571,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Kinetic Theory of Gases","exam":"Engineering","examName":"VITEEE","q":"The molar specific heats of an ideal gas at constant pressure and volume are denoted by $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}$, respectively. If $\\gamma=\\frac{C_{p}}{C_{v}}$ and $\\mathrm{R}$ is the universal gas constant, then $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}$ is equal to","options":["$\\frac{R}{(\\gamma-1)}$","$\\frac{(\\gamma-1)}{R}$","$\\gamma \\mathrm{R}$","$\\frac{1+\\gamma}{1-\\gamma}$"],"answer":0,"solution":" $\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}}-\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}=\\mathrm{R} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}+\\mathrm{R}$\\\\ \\because $\\gamma=\\frac{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}}}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{V}}+\\mathrm{R}}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{V}}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{V}}}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}}+\\frac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}}$\\\\ \\Rightarrow $\\gamma=1+\\frac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}}=\\gamma-1 \\\\ \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\gamma-1} \\end{array}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"VITEEE 2018","_marksId":"64e4593fec226f2f4360aa63"},{"id":117572,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Kinetic Theory of Gases","exam":"Engineering","examName":"VITEEE","q":"Variation of internal energy with density of one mole of monoatomic gas is depicted in the below figure, corresponding variation of pressure with volume can be depicted as (assuming the curve is rectangular hyperbola)\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fviteee%252F